    "q-143482": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143482",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =250$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =64.$ Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $260.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ | $\\,0.9699\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=128,\\,\\mu =250,\\,\\sigma =64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(250,\\frac{{64}^{2}}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(250,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<260).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =P(Z<\\frac{260-250}{\\sqrt{32}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.77) \\\\  & =Φ(1.77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.77)=0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<260) & =Φ(1.77) \\\\  & =0.9616.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
