    "q-143523": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143523",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{84}$ be a random sample of size $n=84$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $1.75.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=84,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0+0.3+0.6+0.6 \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+0.3+1.2+1.8)-2.25 \\\\  & =1.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{1.05}{84}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.0125).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.75).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.75) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{1.75-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.0125}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.24) \\\\  & =Φ(2.24).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.24)=0.9875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.75) & =Φ(2.24) \\\\  & =0.9875.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
