    "q-143505": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143505",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ be a random sample of size $n=20$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.04,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<0.8).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.04.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.75,\\frac{0.04}{20}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<0.8).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<0.8) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.8-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.12)=0.8686.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<0.8) & =Φ(1.12) \\\\  & =0.8686.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
